WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.370
Good working leaves a trail.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.600
If you write what you have established, which cases you

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.590
have finished, and what you will do next, you can pause,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.470
return, or find a mistake without starting again.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.620
Under time pressure, it is easy to repeat work or trust

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.070
an unchecked answer.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.880
Keep a clear trail to show where you stopped, and use an

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.730
independent check to catch errors.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.860
A five by five grid of small squares has all the squares

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.310
along its border shaded.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.210
How many squares are shaded?

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.300
Pause and find the answer in one way, then in a

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.550
second, different way.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:40.360
First method: take away the inside.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.000
The whole grid has twenty-five squares.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.850
The unshaded inner block is three by

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:47.460
three: nine squares.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.220
Twenty-five minus nine is sixteen.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.640
Second method: split the border into four strips of four

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.820
squares, each strip starting at a corner and stopping

00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:59.170
one short of the next.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.350
Four times four is sixteen.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.030
The two methods agree.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.340
A common error is to say four sides of

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:07.870
five squares: twenty.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:09.950
That counts each corner twice.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.070
Subtract the four corners and you get sixteen again.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.440
A second method is valuable precisely because it would

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.490
have caught that mistake.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.020
For longer problems, label your cases as you go,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.050
and tick each one when it is finished.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.500
If you must leave a problem, write one line saying what

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.150
to do next.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.390
Your future self will thank you.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.510
Record what you know and what comes next.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.330
Tick off finished cases.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.380
Then check the answer by a method that could not make

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.370
the same mistake.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.860
Notebook challenge: find the number of border squares of

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.510
a seven by seven grid in two different ways.
